    slug: 'the-plague',
    intro: "Albert Camus's The Plague (1947) is a standalone novel narrating an outbreak of bubonic plague in Oran, a French Algerian city, told through the eyes of Dr. Bernard Rieux as he and a handful of companions fight the epidemic while the city is quarantined. It is Camus's most sustained exploration of solidarity, absurdism, and how human beings respond to collective catastrophe.",
    startWith: 'The Plague',
    books: [
      { title: 'The Plague', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1947, note: 'The complete novel — Oran under quarantine, Dr. Rieux and his companions against the epidemic. Stand-alone.' },
      { title: 'The Stranger', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1942, note: 'Meursault\'s flat account of his own crime and trial — the purest expression of Camus\'s absurdism, written before The Plague. Essential Camus context.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The Fall', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1956, note: 'A former Parisian lawyer confesses his life to a stranger in an Amsterdam bar — Camus\'s most ironic, most difficult novel, his last published in his lifetime.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The Myth of Sisyphus', author: 'Albert Camus', year: 1942, note: 'The philosophical essay that provides the framework for both The Stranger and The Plague — the absurd, rebellion, and the question of suicide.', isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Is The Plague an allegory for the Nazi occupation of France?', a: 'Yes, though Camus always resisted being pinned down to a single allegorical reading. The Plague was written during and immediately after the German occupation of France, and many original readers understood Oran under quarantine as France under occupation, the plague itself as Nazism or totalitarianism, and the various characters as types of response to occupation — collaboration, resistance, passivity, escape. Camus himself said the book had "an obvious content, that of the struggle of the European Resistance movements against Nazism," but also insisted it had broader application to any situation of collective suffering and response. The allegorical reading enriches rather than limits the novel.' },
      { q: 'Should I read The Stranger before The Plague?', a: 'It is not required, but reading The Stranger first is valuable context. The Stranger (1942) and The Plague (1947) represent different phases of Camus\'s philosophical development: The Stranger is about an individual confronting the absurd alone, while The Plague is about collective response to catastrophe and the idea of solidarity as a response to the absurd. The Myth of Sisyphus (1942), his philosophical essay, provides the framework for both. Reading in the order Stranger → Myth of Sisyphus → Plague traces the development of Camus\'s thought most clearly.' },
      { q: 'Why does the narrator of The Plague not identify himself until late in the novel?', a: 'The narrator announces himself as "the chronicle" of the epidemic and maintains a deliberately objective, administrative tone throughout much of the novel, deferring the revelation of his identity to near the end. This is a deliberate narrative strategy: by presenting himself as a collective voice rather than an individual, the narrator aligns himself with the community\'s experience rather than his own particular one. Dr. Rieux\'s decision to speak in the third person about himself (referring to "Rieux" rather than "I" throughout most of the novel) is also a moral stance — a refusal of individual heroism in favor of witnessing. The late revelation of the narrator\'s identity is meant to be felt as both surprise and confirmation.' },
    ],
